{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-115348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ estimate the value of $f(\\sqrt{2})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we have $f(0)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x-0) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(\\sqrt{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{2}) & ≈L(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "445ee9cb19a75e290d309ccd192249c7de6dbdbb26051390f85c636f106e63b3"} {"question" "q-115348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},$ an overestimate"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "445ee9cb19a75e290d309ccd192249c7de6dbdbb26051390f85c636f106e63b3"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2{e}^{4}"] ["math" "4{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-115592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1)$ and $f(1)=3.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(1.3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=3,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)=(1)^{2}(5\\cdot 1+1)=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.3) & ≈L(1.3) \\\\  & =3+6(1.3-1) \\\\  & =3+6(0.3) \\\\  & =3+1.8 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "445ee9cb19a75e290d309ccd192249c7de6dbdbb26051390f85c636f106e63b3"} {"question" "q-314897" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314897" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5,$ estimate the value of $f(1.7)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n$\\,f(1.7)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+1.5(x-2) \\\\  & =3+1.5x-3 \\\\  & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.7)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.7) & ≈L(1.7) \\\\  & =1.5(1.7) \\\\  & =2.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{underestimate}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "445ee9cb19a75e290d309ccd192249c7de6dbdbb26051390f85c636f106e63b3"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.35"] ["math" "2.15"] ["math" "2.55"] ["math" "3.15"] ["math" "3.25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.55"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "overestimate"] ["text" "underestimate"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-314901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "underestimate"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-346054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $f(-1)=2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ to estimate the value of $f(-0.6).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-0.6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-346054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(-1)=2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=(-1)^{3}-1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2-2(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.6) & ≈L(-0.6) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.6+1) \\\\  & =2-2(0.4) \\\\  & =2-0.8 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-346054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "445ee9cb19a75e290d309ccd192249c7de6dbdbb26051390f85c636f106e63b3"} {"question" "q-346054" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"} {"question" "q-346054" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040456/state.json" "file_sha256" "097d3317bb13095bdf9e92ed72aab1dbe38913043feebdbbc24f3e1eed6fc544"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1442 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
